        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(infra): consolidate identifier digest mechanics",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe core still had repeated one-shot SHA-256 mechanics for identifiers, fingerprints, persisted approval markers, and install-path slugs after the shared digest helper was introduced. Those copies made byte-level digest behavior easier to drift while obscuring which parts were generic mechanics versus owner-specific policy.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis routes exact SHA-256 hex, prefix, and base64url mechanics through `src/infra/crypto-digest.ts` while leaving every caller-owned input, prefix, width, persistence/security meaning, and domain name in place. The audit also checked presentation-only short identifiers; no shared truncation helper was added because the remaining sites have distinct trimming, threshold, width, ellipsis, UUID, masking, callback, or artifact-name contracts and would not produce a net-negative abstraction.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo user-visible behavior changes. Identifier formats, persisted approval markers, security-event hashes, OAuth challenges, session generations, and managed install paths remain byte-for-byte compatible.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Focused Vitest proof: 11 explicit test files passed across 6 shards, 621 tests total.\n- Follow-up focused proof: `src/infra/exec-approvals.test.ts`, 8 tests passed.\n- Remote changed gate: Testbox-through-Crabbox `tbx_01kwnhrsbmf3ehyeg9sch6msye`, exit 0; typecheck, test typecheck, lint, guards, and runtime import-cycle check passed.\n- GitHub Actions hydration/run: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/28693150330\n- Fresh Codex autoreview: clean, no accepted/actionable findings, confidence 0.98.\n- Production diff: 64 additions, 65 deletions, net -1 line.\n",
